---
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import {
  Badge,
  Badges,
  Button,
  CodeBlock,
  Facts,
  Faq,
  Features,
  Hero,
  Install,
  Quickstart,
  ReleaseHighlights,
  Spotlight,
} from '@casoon/pages-theme/components';
import Base from '@casoon/pages-theme/layouts/Base.astro';
import config from 'virtual:casoon-pages/config';
import { parseChangelog } from '@casoon/pages-theme/lib/changelog';
import { url } from '@casoon/pages-theme/url';
// Animated SVGs built with castwright from site/captures/judge*.terminal.yaml: excerpts of
// examples/summary.ansi and examples/compare.txt (see site/captures/excerpt.sh). The narrow
// scene has 53 columns, so the text stays readable on phones.
import judgeNarrowSvg from '../../captures/judge-narrow.svg?url';
import judgeSvg from '../../captures/judge.svg?url';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';

const usage = `cargo judge                    # triage report
cargo judge --save-baseline    # accept this state
cargo judge compare .judge/baseline.json`;

const deepTierUsage = `cargo install cargo-judge --features deep
cargo judge inspect                      # "deep: available"

cargo judge dead-code
cargo judge explain my_crate::parser::parse --why-live`;

// Skip "Unreleased": it has no version to show as "New in v…" yet.
const latestRelease = config.changelog
  ? parseChangelog(await readFile(config.changelog, 'utf8')).find(
      (r) => r.version.toLowerCase() !== 'unreleased'
    )
  : undefined;
---

<Base>
  <Hero
    title="Know what a refactoring left behind."
    lead="The refactoring compiles and Clippy is quiet, yet something is off. judge reads your Rust workspace, reports what the change left behind with the evidence for every finding, and tells CI whether it made things worse."
  >
    <Install commands={[{ label: 'crates.io', command: 'cargo install cargo-judge' }]} />
    <Badges>
      <Badge>BUSL-1.1</Badge>
      <Badge>v{config.version}</Badge>
      <Badge>Rust 1.95+</Badge>
      <Badge tone="accent">offline by default</Badge>
    </Badges>
    <picture slot="output">
      <source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={judgeNarrowSvg} width="469.2" height="476" />
      <img
        class="terminal-demo"
        src={judgeSvg}
        width="578.4"
        height="440"
        alt="Terminal, excerpts of two real judge 0.7.0 runs on its own source tree. cargo judge prints the start of the Judge summary: 671 evidence and 333 advisory findings, top rules duplicate-code (635), complexity-inflation (243), integer-cast-risk (39), maintainability-index (39) and panic-in-lib (22). cargo judge compare .judge/baseline.json then prints the count lines: verdict fail, unchanged 934, resolved 45, introduced 56, introduced advisory (no verdict effect) 14, severity changed 0. The full output of both runs is in the showcase."
      />
    </picture>
  </Hero>

  <Features
    heading="Who it is for"
    items={[
      {
        title: 'Rust teams after a big change',
        body: 'You moved modules, split crates or reshaped the API. judge shows what the move left behind, beyond what the compiler and Clippy already report.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Reviewers of generated code',
        body: 'judge reads only the checked-out tree, no Git history or authorship. Code written by people and code written by agents get the same findings.',
      },
      {
        title: 'CI that should not get worse',
        body: 'Save the state you accept as a baseline. Every later run reports what was resolved and what is new, and fails only on new findings that are backed by evidence.',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <Facts
    items={[
      { value: '78', label: 'rules, each with a declared evidence class' },
      { value: '49', label: 'of them can fail a verdict; 29 are advisory only' },
      { value: '4', label: 'output formats: terminal, JSON, SARIF and Markdown' },
      { value: '0', label: 'network calls by default; crates.io checks are opt-in' },
    ]}
  />

  <section class="how" aria-labelledby="how-heading">
    <h2 id="how-heading">How the CI verdict works</h2>
    <div class="text">
      <p>
        Run judge once on a tree you accept and save its findings as a baseline. On every later
        tree, <code>cargo judge compare</code> sorts the findings into unchanged, resolved and
        introduced.
      </p>
      <p>
        Only introduced findings that are backed by evidence and carry warning or failure severity
        fail the verdict and the exit code. Heuristic findings are listed, but never block a
        merge.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Diagram name="baseline-verdict" format="svg" narrow={{ width: 380, height: 700 }} />
    <div class="links">
      <Button href={url('docs/guides/baselines-and-ci/')} variant="secondary">Baselines and CI</Button>
      <Button href={url('docs/reference/rules/')} variant="secondary">All 78 rules</Button>
      <Button href={url('showcase/')} variant="quiet">Real output →</Button>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Spotlight
    eyebrow="Fast tier or deep tier"
    title="One more build step, real reference data"
    lead="**The default Fast Tier needs no build step and reads the tree as written.** Compiling in rust-analyzer itself, behind the `deep` Cargo feature, lets `dead-code` and `explain --why-live` work from real reference data instead of syntax-level guesses."
    points={[
      '**Dead code, with evidence.** `dead-code` reports `unused-pub-workspace` — no reference from another workspace crate and no reachability from a recognised entry point, not proof.',
      '**Why something is still live.** `explain --why-live` prints the shortest evidenced route from `fn main`, a test or an FFI boundary to the item, each edge classified as static, dynamic, macro, generated or unknown.',
      'The build cost is upfront, not per run. Compiling the `ra_ap_*` crates takes noticeably longer once; `cargo judge inspect` confirms `deep: available` afterward.',
    ]}
    links={[
      { label: 'Deep Tier guide', href: url('docs/guides/deep-tier/') },
      { label: 'All 78 rules', href: url('docs/reference/rules/') },
    ]}
  >
    <CodeBlock code={deepTierUsage} lang="sh" label="Deep Tier" />
  </Spotlight>

  <Quickstart
    lead="Install it, run it in the workspace root, and turn the result into a baseline for CI. Crate boundaries in judge.toml, the output formats and the rust-analyzer deep tier are covered in the documentation."
    steps={[
      'Install <code>cargo-judge</code> from crates.io with the line above.',
      'Run <code>cargo judge</code> in the workspace root for the grouped triage report.',
      'Save a baseline and let CI run <code>cargo judge compare</code> against it.',
    ]}
  >
    <CodeBlock code={usage} lang="sh" label="Three commands" />
  </Quickstart>

  {latestRelease && <ReleaseHighlights release={latestRelease} />}

  <Faq
    items={[
      {
        q: "What does judge check that Clippy and the compiler don't?",
        a: 'Structural and cross-file concerns that only become visible across a workspace — duplicated code, complexity concentration, public API surface and dependency hygiene. Anything the compiler or Clippy already tells you, judge does not repeat.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Does judge look at Git history or who wrote the code?',
        a: 'No. It analyzes only the current source tree, workspace structure and dependency graph, never commit history, authorship or telemetry, so human-written and agent-refactored code get the same findings.',
      },
      {
        q: 'How does the CI verdict work?',
        a: 'Save a baseline from a tree you accept with --save-baseline, then run cargo judge compare against it in CI. Only new findings backed by evidence and warning or failure severity fail the verdict; heuristic findings are listed but never block a merge.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What is the Deep Tier, and do I need it?',
        a: 'The default Fast Tier needs no build step. The Deep Tier, behind the deep Cargo feature, loads the workspace into rust-analyzer for dead-code and why-live analysis, at the cost of a noticeably longer build.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Does judge make network calls?',
        a: 'Not by default. Dependency checks against crates.io, such as phantom or yanked dependencies, are opt-in via --check-crates-io, and judge never runs cargo-audit itself.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  /* Never scale the SVG beyond a readable terminal size. */
  .terminal-demo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 720px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .how {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 24px 96px;
  }
  h2 {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    color: var(--heading);
    font-size: 30px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .text {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(320px, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 0 48px;
  }
  .text p {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
  }
</style>
